R=[f'anneal-{t}' for t in [600,700,800,900,1000]]
rows('precursors',[
('lanthanum-oxide','Lanthanum oxide','Solution A uses 1.1760 g La₂O₃, reported as 3.607 mmol and 99.99% purity. The rare-earth oxides are supplied by Aldrich. La₂O₃ provides the host rare-earth component; exact dissolved nitrate hydration/coordination is not characterized.',2,'Section 2.1',['stock-a'],'method_context'),
('ytterbium-oxide','Ytterbium oxide','Solution A uses 0.3692 g Yb₂O₃, reported as 0.937 mmol and 99.99% purity, from Aldrich. Yb is the sensitizing dopant in the proposed phosphor mechanism. The paper does not measure the final incorporated Yb concentration separately from the precursor composition.',2,'Section 2.1',['stock-a'],'method_context'),
('erbium-oxide','Erbium oxide','Solution A uses 0.05370 g Er₂O₃, reported as 0.140 mmol and 99.99% purity, from Aldrich. Er is the emitting dopant. The separate Er concentration series does not provide new weighed charges for every composition.',2,'Section 2.1',['stock-a','erbium-series'],'method_context'),
('nitric-acid','Dilute nitric acid','Dilute nitric acid dissolves the rare-earth oxides. The solution is warmed to dryness to remove unreacted acid before redissolving the residue. Acid molarity, volume, excess, drying temperature and endpoint assay are not supplied.',2,'Section 2.1',['stock-a'],'method_context'),
('water','Deionized water','The dried Solution A residue is redissolved in 30 mL deionized water. Solution B separately uses 30 mL deionized water. Deionized water also washes the recovered precipitate twice; wash volumes are unreported. No organic solvent or surfactant is specified.',2,'Section 2.1',['stock-a','stock-b']+R,'method_context'),
('ammonium-molybdate','Printed ammonium molybdate charge','Solution B uses 1.961 g (NH₄)₂MoO₄, reported as 9.37 mmol, from Beijing Chemical Corp. Purity and hydration state are not given. The printed formula, mass and amount are preserved as a source inconsistency rather than silently substituting a different molybdate formula or corrected dose.',2,'Section 2.1',['stock-b'],'curator_interpretation','source_evidence_conflict'),
('mass-amount-check','Curator check: molybdate mass and amount','Dividing the printed 1.961 g by 9.37 mmol implies approximately 209.3 g/mol, whereas the literal anhydrous (NH₄)₂MoO₄ formula is approximately 196.0 g/mol. This arithmetic check identifies an unresolved source inconsistency; it does not prove a hydrate identity or authorize a corrected mass, amount or concentration.',2,'Section 2.1, printed formula and charge',['stock-b'],'curator_interpretation','curator_arithmetic_check'),
('feed-ratio-check','Curator check: feed and target stoichiometry','The reported oxide amounts correspond to 9.368 mmol total rare-earth atoms. The printed 9.37 mmol Mo amount gives an approximately 1:1 Mo-to-rare-earth feed ratio, while the nominal La₂(MoO₄)₃ host has a 3:2 ratio. This consistency check is not evidence for a specific off-stoichiometric product, reaction yield or corrected recipe. All source charges remain unchanged.',2,'Section 2.1, charge list and target formula',['stock-a','stock-b'],'curator_interpretation','curator_arithmetic_check'),
('molybdenum-trioxide','Bulk-route molybdenum precursor','The bulk reference is made from La₂O₃, MoO₃, Yb₂O₃ and Er₂O₃ powders. MoO₃ belongs to this solid-state comparison and does not replace ammonium molybdate in the hydrothermal method. Absolute bulk charges and Mo-to-rare-earth ratio are not supplied.',2,'Bulk comparison paragraph',['bulk'],'method_context'),
('stock-a-composition','Solution A composition','After acid dissolution and drying, the combined La/Yb/Er-containing residue is redissolved in 30 mL deionized water and stirred for 1 h at room temperature. This is the reported added water volume, not an independently measured final solution volume; exact speciation, pH and final concentrations are not stated.',2,'Section 2.1',['stock-a'],'method_context'),
('stock-b-composition','Solution B composition','Dissolve the printed ammonium molybdate charge in 30 mL deionized water and stir for 1 h at room temperature. The mass–amount conflict prevents treating a calculated stock molarity as an unambiguous source value. No pH or dissolved polymolybdate distribution is measured.',2,'Section 2.1',['stock-b'],'method_context'),
('dopant-basis','Dopant composition and incorporation','For the bulk comparison the stated La:Yb:Er molar ratio is 77:20:3. The hydrothermal charges and the separate Er mole-fraction series retain their own source bases. Nominal precursor composition is not an elemental-analysis measurement of incorporated ions or an exact doped unit-cell formula.',2,'Section 2.1',['bulk','stock-a','erbium-series'],'curator_interpretation','curation_scope'),
])
rows('protocol',[
('acid-dissolution','Dissolve the rare-earth oxides','Dissolve the three reported oxide charges in dilute nitric acid to prepare Solution A. Addition order, acid volume and concentration, reaction time and temperature during dissolution are unspecified.',2,'Section 2.1',['stock-a'],'method_context'),
('acid-removal','Evaporate unreacted acid','Warm the oxide/acid solution to dryness to remove unreacted nitric acid. No specific heater, temperature, duration or evidence of fully acid-free residue is provided.',2,'Section 2.1',['stock-a'],'method_context'),
('a-redissolution','Redissolve and stir Solution A','Redissolve the residue in 30 mL deionized water and stir for 1 h at room temperature. The paper gives no numerical room temperature or stirring speed.',2,'Section 2.1',['stock-a'],'method_context'),
('b-dissolution','Prepare and stir Solution B','Dissolve 1.961 g of the printed (NH₄)₂MoO₄ reagent, described as 9.37 mmol, in 30 mL deionized water. Stir for 1 h at room temperature. The source charge inconsistency remains explicit.',2,'Section 2.1',['stock-b'],'method_context'),
('dropwise-addition','Add Solution B to Solution A','Add Solution B drop by drop to Solution A at 20–30 drops/min with vigorous stirring. Addition direction is specified. Drop volume, total addition time, mixing rpm and pH are not given, so drops/min is not converted to mL/min.',2,'Section 2.1',R,'method_context'),
('post-addition','Stir the resulting suspension','Continue stirring the formed suspension for 20 min. The identity and composition of this initial precipitate are not independently resolved in the source; it is not assigned a verified final crystalline phase at this stage.',2,'Section 2.1',R,'method_context'),
('autoclave-charge','Transfer and close the hydrothermal vessel','Transfer the suspension into a 100 mL Teflon vessel, cap it and place it in an RD-100 autoclave from the Institute of Beijing Petrochemical Industry. Vessel capacity is not a measured liquid fill volume. No numerical pressure or headspace composition is supplied.',2,'Section 2.1',R,'method_context'),
('hydrothermal-treatment','Hydrothermal treatment','Heat the loaded autoclave to 180 °C for 1 h. The source does not give the heating ramp, actual pressure, cooling schedule or the criterion used to start the hold time. Subsequent annealing-temperature variants share the reported wet-chemical framework without proving identical physical batches.',2,'Section 2.1',R,'method_context'),
('centrifugation','Collect the precipitate','Centrifuge the suspension at 6,000 rpm for 10 min. The precipitate is retained. Rotor radius and relative centrifugal force are unreported; rpm is not converted to g. The source does not quantify centrifugation temperature or liquid-removal losses.',2,'Section 2.1',R,'method_context'),
('washing','Wash twice with deionized water','Wash the collected precipitate twice with deionized water. Wash volume, resuspension method and any repeat centrifugation conditions are not separately stated.',2,'Section 2.1',R,'method_context'),
('drying','Air-dry the precipitate','Dry the washed precipitate in air to obtain a white powder. Drying temperature and duration, mass yield and storage conditions are not supplied. Air drying is not evidence that the later nanocrystal anneal also used a specified air atmosphere.',2,'Section 2.1',R,'method_context'),
('anneal-800','Reference nanocrystal anneal at 800 °C','Heat the nanocrystals to 800 °C at 20 °C/min, hold for 5 h and allow natural cooling to room temperature. The final product is a white powder. This explicit ramp and cooling description belongs to the stated 800 °C method; it is not automatically verified for every comparison temperature.',2,'Section 2.1',['anneal-800'],'method_context'),
('annealing-series','Five annealing-temperature conditions','The comparison treats nanocrystals for 5 h at 600, 700, 800, 900 and 1,000 °C, as labeled in Figure 6. The authors choose 800 °C for subsequent experiments as a compromise between strong emission and retained nanoscale size. Separate batch amounts and variant-specific ramps/cooling are not supplied.',3,'Annealing discussion and Figure 6',R,'method_context'),
('bulk-mix','Mix the bulk-reference powders','Mix La₂O₃, MoO₃, Yb₂O₃ and Er₂O₃ in the solid state, with a reported La:Yb:Er ratio of 77:20:3. The source cites reference 15 but provides no absolute masses, grinding duration or total Mo loading.',2,'Bulk comparison paragraph',['bulk'],'method_context'),
('bulk-press','Press a pellet','Press the bulk powder mixture into a pellet. Die size, pressure, dwell time, binder and pellet mass are not supplied.',2,'Bulk comparison paragraph',['bulk'],'method_context'),
('bulk-fire','Fire the bulk reference','Fire the pellet in air at 1,200 °C for 5 h. Heating ramp and cooling schedule are unspecified. The bulk route is distinct from the hydrothermal nanocrystal route and from the 1,000 °C annealed nanocrystal-derived material.',2,'Bulk comparison paragraph',['bulk'],'method_context'),
('bulk-grinding','Qualitative bulk-grinding comparison','The authors state that grinding a bulk sample into a very fine powder did not improve its optical properties. No grinding equipment, time, energy, final particle size or complete spectrum is supplied; this is an incomplete comparison procedure rather than a quantified alternative nanocrystal synthesis.',5,'Grinding paragraph',['bulk-grinding'],'method_context'),
('tem-method','TEM acquisition','TEM images are collected with a Hitachi instrument. The source does not specify model, accelerating voltage, grid preparation, particle count or image-analysis protocol. Figure 2 shows before-annealing and 800 °C/5 h states.',2,'Section 2.2',['tem'],'method_context'),
('xrd-method','X-ray diffraction acquisition and analysis','Use a Bruker D8 advance diffractometer. The reported analysis compares peak positions with ICDD 45-0407 and estimates crystallite size from measured and instrumental peak widths using Scherrer’s equation. Radiation wavelength, shape factor, scan step and exact broadening correction are not supplied.',2,'Section 2.2 and XRD discussion',['xrd'],'method_context'),
('sizing-method','Particle-size analysis','Particle sizing uses a BI-90Plus analyzer from Brookhaven Instruments. The source does not give dispersion medium, concentration, refractive-index assumptions or a conversion from intensity to number distribution. Figure 3’s ordinate is Intensity, not a counted-particle percentage.',2,'Section 2.2',['particle-size'],'method_context'),
('fluorescence-method','Fluorescence acquisition','Fluorescence is recorded with a Perkin-Elmer LS-50B spectrophotometer using the front-surface accessory. Down-conversion excitation is 374 nm. Acquisition temperature, slit widths, integration time and absolute quantum-yield calibration are unreported.',2,'Section 2.2',['downconversion','upconversion'],'method_context'),
('upconversion-method','980 nm up-conversion excitation','For up-conversion spectra, an external 980 nm laser from Beijing Hi-Tech Optoelectronic Co., specified as 50 mW, replaces the spectrophotometer’s xenon source and is coupled through an optical-fiber accessory. Beam area and delivered irradiance are not stated; 50 mW is not an intensity in W/cm².',2,'Section 2.2 continuation',['upconversion','power-response'],'method_context'),
('near-ir-method','Near-infrared absorption acquisition','Near-IR spectra use a Perkin-Elmer PE SYSTEM 2000 FTIR spectrophotometer with a quartz beam splitter. The measured wavenumber range is 9,000–11,000 cm⁻¹. The original plot’s absorbance direction is retained.',2,'Section 2.2',['near-ir'],'method_context'),
('erbium-method','Erbium mole-fraction series','The annealed-nanocrystal study varies the stated Er³⁺ mole fraction over 1–7%. Figure 7 contains visible points at 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 7%; no 6% point is plotted. Exact modified oxide charges, the denominator definition and which other components were held fixed are not fully specified.',3,'Er concentration discussion',['erbium-series'],'method_context'),
('power-method','Excitation-power comparison','Measure the up-conversion response under changing 980 nm excitation and plot ln(I_up) against ln(I_exc) for three emission bands. No absolute excitation-intensity calibration, logarithm reference scale, beam spot or individual raw-data table is provided.',3,'Power discussion and Figure 8',['power-response'],'method_context'),
])
more('near-ir-method',3,'Near-IR spectral window');more('fluorescence-method',3,'374 nm excitation');more('annealing-series',4,'Figure 6 labels');more('erbium-method',4,'Figure 7 visible points')
rows('structures',[
('phase','Tetragonal host with a minor second phase','The 800 °C/5 h XRD pattern is assigned to crystalline tetragonal La₂(MoO₄)₃ with a small second phase and matches ICDD 45-0407 and the bulk reference according to the authors. The minor phase identity and phase fractions are not given. The sample must not be labeled phase-pure.',2,'XRD discussion',['xrd','anneal-800']),
('xrd-widths','Reported peak position and widths','For the strongest reflection the source gives 2θ = 28.053, a measured FWHM B₁ = 0.186° and instrumental width B₀ = 0.104°. These are inputs to the reported Scherrer estimate; no guessed reflection index, corrected width or lattice parameter is supplied.',2,'XRD discussion',['xrd']),
('scherrer-size','Scherrer crystallite-size estimate','The authors report a crystallite size of 52.5 nm from Scherrer analysis. The full equation, radiation wavelength, shape factor and correction implementation are not printed. This derived coherent-domain estimate is distinct from TEM diameter and particle-size-analyzer output.',2,'XRD discussion',['xrd']),
('tem-morphology','Morphology from TEM','Figure 2 is described as showing nearly spherical nanocrystals, with most diameters in the 40–60 nm range and particles distinguishable from one another. The original images, including local contacts and clustering, are retained rather than replaced by an idealized monodisperse arrangement.',2,'TEM discussion',['tem']),
('tem-scales','TEM states and scale bars','Figure 2a is before annealing and has a 300 nm scale bar. Figure 2b is after 800 °C for 5 h and has a 100 nm bar. These are separate source states with different displayed magnifications; identical specimen identity or unchanged individual particles is not established.',2,'Figure 2',['tem']),
('size-distribution','Analyzer distribution and average diameter','The text says most particles are 45–65 nm with average diameter approximately 53 nm. Figure 3 plots an intensity distribution with lower-size bars as well; it is not a TEM number histogram. The abstract/conclusion round the characteristic diameter to 50 nm.',2,'Size-distribution discussion',['particle-size']),
('single-crystal-inference','Author inference of single-crystal particles','The authors compare the approximately 52.5 nm coherent-domain estimate with particle dimensions to argue for single-crystal particles rather than aggregates of much smaller crystallites. This is a cross-technique inference, not a displayed lattice-resolved TEM or SAED determination.',2,'Final characterization paragraph',['xrd','tem','particle-size'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('anneal-size','Size evolution during heat treatment','The source states that particle size is retained at 800 °C, that growth and aggregation occur at 900 °C, and that after 1,000 °C for 5 h the material becomes bulk-like. No exact 900/1,000 °C size or corresponding original TEM images are supplied; Figure 2b only shows the 800 °C state.',3,'Annealing discussion',['anneal-800','anneal-900','anneal-1000']),
('structure-limit','Unreported atomistic structure','The article does not supply refined lattice constants, site occupancies, atom coordinates, a CIF, a SAED pattern or a direct dopant-distribution map. A host chemical formula and ICDD reference cannot establish a measured, dopant-resolved structure suitable as a DFT ground truth.',2,'XRD/TEM results and complete main article',[],'curator_interpretation','reviewed_source_limitation'),
])
more('size-distribution',3,'Figure 3');more('size-distribution',1,'Abstract');more('size-distribution',5,'Conclusion')
rows('properties',[
('white-powder','Product appearance','White powders are obtained after washing/air drying and after the specified 800 °C anneal. No colorimetric coordinates, isolated mass yield or storage stability test is provided.',2,'Section 2.1',['anneal-800']),
('downconversion-peaks','Down-conversion emission','Under 374 nm excitation the nanocrystals emit at approximately 525 and 549 nm. The authors assign the bands to Er³⁺ ²H₁₁/₂→⁴I₁₅/₂ and ⁴S₃/₂→⁴I₁₅/₂, respectively. These down-conversion wavelengths remain separate from the shifted up-conversion bands.',3,'Figure 4a discussion',['downconversion']),
('upconversion-peaks','Up-conversion emission','Under 980 nm excitation the reported emission peaks are 519, 541 and 653 nm, assigned to ²H₁₁/₂, ⁴S₃/₂ and ⁴F₉/₂ decays to the ⁴I₁₅/₂ ground state. The spectrum is green-dominated with a weak red feature in the nanocrystal sample.',3,'Figure 4b discussion',['upconversion']),
('figure4','Figure 4: separate optical experiments','Panel a contains a dotted excitation spectrum and solid down-conversion emission spectrum, with separate wavelength regions in one plot. Panel b is the 980 nm pumped up-conversion spectrum. Both ordinates are arbitrary intensity; excitation and emission traces are not an absolute conversion-efficiency calibration.',3,'Figure 4',['downconversion','upconversion']),
('nir-band','Near-IR absorption maximum and range','The absorption is reported near 10,238 cm⁻¹, corresponding to the source-rounded 976 nm. Its stated band range is 10,625–9,875 cm⁻¹, given as 941–1,013 nm, and is used to estimate a suitable excitation interval. These source-rounded wavelength/wavenumber values are retained without silently replacing them.',3,'Figure 5 discussion',['near-ir']),
('nir-assignment','Assignment of the near-IR absorption','The band is attributed to Er³⁺ ⁴I₁₅/₂→⁴I₁₁/₂ and Yb³⁺ ²F₇/₂→²F₅/₂ transitions. The source does not provide separately deconvolved Yb and Er absorption cross sections.',3,'Near-IR discussion',['near-ir'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('figure5-axis','Figure 5: original absorbance axis','The plot is labeled Absorbance, with 0 at the top and 1 at the bottom; the wavenumber axis decreases from 11,000 to 9,000 cm⁻¹. This unusual presentation is shown unchanged and is not relabeled as transmittance or numerically inverted into a new spectrum.',4,'Figure 5',['near-ir'],'curator_interpretation','curation_scope'),
('before-anneal','Weak emission before annealing','The wet-chemically prepared nanocrystals are reported to have very weak fluorescence before annealing, attributed to poor crystallinity. No exact unannealed intensity, phase-fraction measurement or time-resolved crystallization series is supplied.',3,'Annealing discussion',['mechanisms'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('anneal-emission','Emission versus annealing temperature','Emission increases strongly from 600 to 800 °C and more slowly from 800 to 900 °C, then decreases sharply at 1,000 °C, all after 5 h. At 1,000 °C the 541 nm peak exceeds the 519 nm peak. The authors choose 800 °C despite the 900 °C intensity increase because nanoscale structure is better retained.',3,'Annealing discussion',R),
('figure6','Figure 6: five discrete anneals','The original spectra label a–e as 600, 700, 800, 900 and 1,000 °C, each for 5 h, under 980 nm laser-diode pumping. The plot spans 450–700 nm and an arbitrary-intensity axis around 4–10. It is a discrete comparison, not a continuous temperature-to-emission predictor.',4,'Figure 6',R),
('erbium-trend','Reported Er concentration trend','The body states that all three up-conversion peaks increase from 1 to 3% Er³⁺ and decrease sharply over 4–7%, attributed to concentration quenching. It selects 3% as optimum. The formulation denominator and final incorporated Er content are not analytically verified.',3,'Er series discussion',['erbium-series']),
('erbium-conflict','Figure 7 versus the concentration-trend prose','Figure 7 shows one unassigned intensity curve, not three independently labeled band curves. The 4% point remains close to the 3% maximum, while the sharp drop is visible at 5%; this differs from the prose’s 4–7% decline. A 6% value is not plotted. The original graph and prose remain distinct; no exact missing intensities are reconstructed.',4,'Figure 7 and p. 3 prose',['erbium-series'],'curator_interpretation','source_evidence_conflict'),
('power-law','Up-conversion intensity relation','The source writes I_up ∝ (I_exc)^n, where I_up is visible up-conversion intensity, I_exc is near-IR excitation intensity and n is interpreted as the photon number required per up-converted photon. The observed exponents support a two-photon mechanism; the relation is not a synthesis-rate law.',3,'Power-law paragraph',['power-response'],'model_context','author_analysis'),
('power-slopes','Reported logarithmic slopes','The body gives slopes 2.20, 1.89 and 2.09 for the 519, 541 and 653 nm bands. Figure 8 prints the more precise fit values 2.2024, 1.8853 and 2.0907, respectively. Both source precision levels are preserved; fit uncertainty and raw pairs are unreported.',3,'Power discussion and Figure 8',['power-response']),
('power-label-conflict','519 nm versus 520 nm in Figure 8','The Figure 8 legend and body identify the first series as 519 nm, while the caption calls it 520 nm. The discrepancy is retained as source labeling; it is not a fourth independently measured emission peak.',4,'Figure 8 caption and legend',['power-response'],'curator_interpretation','source_evidence_conflict'),
('figure8','Figure 8: axes and symbol assignments','The graph uses ln(I_exc) horizontally and ln(I_up) vertically. Squares, circles and triangles correspond to the 519, 541 and 653 nm legend labels. Horizontal ticks span approximately 2.0–4.0 and vertical ticks 2–8; these logarithmic display values are not unambiguously calibrated absolute powers or photon fluxes.',4,'Figure 8',['power-response']),
('bulk-spectrum','Bulk versus nanocrystal emission','Figure 10 compares the solid nanocrystal spectrum and dotted bulk spectrum under 980 nm excitation. The nanocrystal ordering is 519 > 541 > 653 nm intensity, whereas the bulk ordering is reversed. The source reports stronger nanocrystal luminescence, but does not provide an absolute quantum yield, mass-normalized absorption correction or numerical enhancement factor.',4,'Bulk comparison, continued on p. 5',['upconversion','bulk']),
('figure10','Figure 10: comparison scales','The original bulk/nanocrystal comparison spans 400–750 nm with an arbitrary-intensity ordinate labeled to 900. The bulk comparator is the separately prepared solid-state reference, not automatically the 1,000 °C annealed nanoparticle-derived product.',5,'Figure 10',['upconversion','bulk']),
('grinding-result','Bulk grinding does not reproduce the enhancement','The authors report that mechanically grinding bulk material into a fine powder did not improve its optical properties. They attribute this to altered intrinsic point-defect concentrations reducing emission. No dedicated grinding spectrum, particle-size distribution or defect assay is displayed.',5,'Grinding discussion',['bulk-grinding'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
])
more('power-slopes',4,'Figure 8 exact legend');more('bulk-spectrum',5,'Figure 10 and discussion')
rows('intuition',[
('phosphor-context','Up-conversion versus down-conversion','Down-conversion emits lower-energy photons after higher-energy excitation; the introduction gives ZnS:Mn and Y₂O₃:Eu examples. Up-conversion emits higher-energy photons from lower-energy excitation and requires at least two photons in the process discussed here. Cited materials are not additional syntheses in this paper.',1,'Introduction, references 1–9',['mechanisms'],'cited_context'),
('application-context','Motivation for luminescent nanocrystals','The introduction discusses solid-state lasers, IR-pumped visible sources and GaAs LEDs coated with up-conversion phosphors. These are cited application contexts, not devices demonstrated using the present material.',1,'Introduction, reference 10',['mechanisms'],'cited_context'),
('bioassay-context','Why pursue nanosized phosphors?','The authors propose bioassay and biochip labels, citing low background, reduced photobleaching and multiplexed visible colors relative to organic cyanine labels such as Cy-3, Cy-5 and Cy-7. They seek narrow nanoscale distributions for DNA, RNA and protein labeling. No conjugation, biological assay, toxicity or photobleaching test is reported here.',1,'Introduction, references 11–14',['mechanisms'],'cited_context'),
('sensitizer-emitter','Yb sensitization and Er emission','The source identifies Yb as the absorber and Er as the emitter in the host lattice: 980 nm excitation is absorbed and energy transferred to Er. This is the proposed sensitizer–activator framework; no spatial dopant distribution or transfer rate is measured.',2,'Introduction continuation',['mechanisms'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('anneal-rationale','Balance crystallinity and particle growth','The authors attribute weak as-made emission to poor crystallinity and the improvement on annealing to better phosphor performance. Higher temperatures ultimately cause growth/aggregation and reduced surface contribution. The selected 800 °C treatment is a reported compromise, not a universal optimum for all formulations.',3,'Annealing discussion',['mechanisms'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('quenching','Concentration quenching interpretation','The reported high-Er decline is attributed to concentration quenching. The graph/prose mismatch at 4% and single unassigned plotted response remain unresolved. No transfer-distance calculation, decay lifetime or quenching rate is supplied.',3,'Er series discussion',['mechanisms','erbium-series'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('two-photon-model','Proposed two-step excitation','Under 980 nm excitation Er³⁺ is described as reaching ⁴I₁₁/₂ from ⁴I₁₅/₂ and then ⁴F₇/₂; nonradiative relaxation populates the emitting ²H₁₁/₂, ⁴S₃/₂ and ⁴F₉/₂ states. Their decays to the ground state produce 519, 541 and 653 nm bands. This model is supported by near-quadratic power dependence but is not a direct time-resolved state-population measurement.',4,'Figure 9 discussion',['mechanisms'],'model_context','author_model'),
('figure9-model','Figure 9: energy-level schematic','The original diagram includes Yb³⁺ ²F₇/₂ and ²F₅/₂, an energy-transfer arrow to Er, and Er ⁴I₁₅/₂, ⁴I₁₃/₂, ⁴I₁₁/₂, ⁴I₉/₂, ⁴F₉/₂, ⁴S₃/₂, ²H₁₁/₂ and ⁴F₇/₂ levels. Solid/dashed arrows show proposed excitation, relaxation and emission; no numerical energy-axis calibration or measured wavefunctions are supplied.',4,'Figure 9',['mechanisms'],'model_context','author_model'),
('bulk-population','Interpretation of the bulk red emission','The source infers that stronger bulk 653 nm emission reflects greater population of the lower ⁴F₉/₂ state and a greater probability of relaxation into it. This is an interpretation of steady-state spectra, not a measured branching ratio or population kinetics.',4,'Bulk discussion',['mechanisms'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('bulk-structure-claim','Scope of the nano–bulk structural comparison','The authors state that the nanocrystal and bulk samples share composition and crystal structure and therefore attribute their spectral differences to size. No quantitative chemical-composition assay, separately refined bulk diffraction pattern or complete control for optical normalization is supplied. The same-structure statement remains an author comparison rather than a verified equality of all material variables.',5,'Size-dependence discussion',['mechanisms','bulk'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('surface-rationale','Proposed surface enhancement','The authors argue that smaller particles expose more luminescent ions while those ions remain part of the lattice. Weaker environmental interactions from surface pendent bonds are proposed to lengthen the Er ⁴I₁₁/₂ lifetime and improve up-conversion. No surface-ion count, measured lifetime or bond characterization is supplied.',5,'Surface-effect interpretation',['mechanisms'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('surface-bands','Surface and interior band assignments','The source tentatively associates 519 nm emission mainly with surface luminescent ions and 653 nm mainly with interior ions, using this to explain the size-dependent spectral shape. This is not a directly mapped distribution of emission sites or an experimentally separated surface spectrum.',5,'Surface-effect interpretation',['mechanisms'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('size-context','Earlier explanations in down-conversion materials','The discussion cites enhanced down-conversion in Y₂O₃:Eu, Y₂O₃:Tb and ZnS:Mn and earlier quantum-confinement or surface-area explanations. Those cited systems and mechanisms do not establish quantum confinement as measured in the present rare-earth phosphor.',5,'Size-dependence discussion',['mechanisms'],'cited_context'),
('grinding-intuition','Why grinding is not equivalent to synthesis','The authors propose that grinding changes point-defect concentrations and can reduce emission, citing Eu-doped CaS and rare-earth oxysulfide examples. Those references are context; the present grinding experiment lacks a quantified defect or size analysis.',5,'Grinding discussion',['mechanisms'],'model_context','author_interpretation'),
('historical-claims','Attributed historical and application claims','The authors describe the nanocrystal synthesis and up-conversion particle-size effect as first observations and propose bio-labeling uses. These are attributed 2002 claims, not a new priority review or demonstrated clinical/biological application.',1,'Abstract and introduction',['mechanisms'],'cited_context','attributed_historical_claim'),
])
rows('sources',[
('identity','Source identity','Guangshun Yi, Baoquan Sun, Fengzhen Yang, Depu Chen, Yuxiang Zhou and Jing Cheng, Synthesis and Characterization of High-Efficiency Nanocrystal Up-Conversion Phosphors: Ytterbium and Erbium Codoped Lanthanum Molybdate, Chemistry of Materials 2002, 14(7), 2910–2914, DOI 10.1021/cm0115416. Received October 25, 2001; revised March 29, 2002; online June 19, 2002.',1,'Title, byline, header and footer',[],'source_metadata','source_metadata'),
('source-context','Affiliations and provenance','The authors list Tsinghua University chemistry and biological-science departments, Beijing National Biochip Research & Engineering Center, and Zibo University. Depu Chen is corresponding author. The July 7, 2026 download watermark and file metadata are administrative, not synthesis or publication dates.',1,'Affiliations, correspondence and watermark',[],'source_metadata','source_metadata'),
('coverage','Supplied-source coverage','All five main pages, ten figures, captions, methods, analysis and 27 cited entries were read and visually inspected. No SI declaration was found in the supplied article and no matching local SI is asserted. A main-only review does not establish that SI cannot exist.',5,'Complete supplied main article',[],'source_metadata','reviewed_source_limitation'),
('missing-fields','Unreported procedural details','Missing details include nitric-acid concentration/volume, drying conditions, solution pH, drop volume, hydrothermal pressure/cooling, wash volumes, isolated yield, annealing atmosphere for the nanocrystal route, variant-specific ramps and complete Er-series formulations. They remain missing rather than borrowed from other papers.',2,'Experimental section',[],'curator_interpretation','reviewed_source_limitation'),
('sample-boundaries','Specimen and variant boundaries','The five annealing states, Er-concentration series, before/after TEM images, particle-size-analyzer distribution, nanocrystal optical studies and separately prepared bulk reference remain scoped comparisons. Shared nominal composition does not establish identical physical batches. The 800 °C primary protocol does not fully specify every concentration variant.',3,'Results and comparison series',[],'curator_interpretation','curation_scope'),
('data-precision','Source figures and numeric precision','Original spectra, microscopy, histogram, point plots and energy diagram retain their axes and source labels. Exact text quantities and precise slope annotations are recorded; arbitrary plotted amplitudes, missing concentration points, raw bin counts, phase fractions and error bars are not invented.',4,'Figures 5–9',[],'curator_interpretation','curation_scope'),
('reference-scope','External references and method dependency','All 27 bibliography entries are retained as printed. Reference 15 is a cited bulk-preparation dependency and reference 16 supports phase comparison; external full texts and ICDD data were not independently inspected in this review. Their recipes and crystal coordinates are not implicitly imported.',2,'References 15–16',[],'cited_context','source_dependency'),
('funding','Research support','The paper acknowledges National Science Foundation of China support under 39989001 and 39825108 and the National Key Basic Research Development Program under G19990116. These identifiers are administrative provenance.',5,'Acknowledgment',[],'source_metadata','source_metadata'),
])
